Yes, I do also agree with the other possible solution you proposed, that was the other way to go I was thinking about. I will think about both solution, reason if there are any unwanted side effects and implement one of them. As soon as I have a PR, I will let you know here!

EDIT: @gfrancesco I've reasoned about the option you proposed and think that it will not work in some edge-cases. There are sensors that reset to 0 during the lifespan of a utility meter cycle (for example those smart plugs without an internally stored cumulative energy reading that reset to 0 after power cycling). If we always calculate the difference between the current reading and the one from the start of the meter cycle, we will have 0 as result until the energy reading exceeds the value before the reset (which may takes hours/ days/ etc.). If you  calculate the difference with the previous valid state, you won't have this problem because the state change to 0 is ignored (because the delta is negative), but from the second reading onwards everything is fine and is collected again.

@dgomes do you perhaps have any feedback on my proposed solution? Are there edge cases we don't see and cover? Many thanks in advance for considering this!
